# Story: a developer docks Xcode, which refuses to get smaller than 1200x600,
# into a narrow slot. Instead of overlapping its neighbours it becomes a tile;
# clicking the tile zooms onto it at full size.
# (Until F61 the story was told with 900x600. Arbor now finds that much room by moving the
# gaps, see 61-room-before-tiles.sim; 1200 is more than the neighbours can spare.)
# Reference: T20 T50 T51. Window ids start at 101; node ids are deterministic.
# As Arbor ships, what cannot fit overlaps (F69). This story is about the tile, which is an option now.
set windowsThatDoNotFit tile
open Safari
open Notes
open Xcode 700x500 min 1200x600
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
dock 103 beside 102 bottom
expect docked 103
expect tile 103
expect visible 102
# 103's leaf is n4 (n1 = 101, n2 = root split, n3 = 102, n4 = 103, n5 = column).
tile n4
expect camera 103
expect fills 103
expect hidden 101
# (Changed by the swarm, 2026-10-15; the documenter may edit it. Until then the story ended with `show-all`, `expect tile 103`.)
# Since the click Xcode has the keyboard, and the window being typed in is never the tile if it fits at all (F61, QUESTIONS 2026-10-15):
# at Show Everything it is a window, at its neighbours' cost: Safari is the tile for as long. Notes above it still fits, so it stays.
show-all
expect visible 103
expect focused 103
expect size 103 1200x600
expect tile 101
expect visible 102
# Back in Safari everything is as it was: what the neighbours lent has gone back.
focus 101
show-all
expect tile 103
expect visible 101
expect visible 102
